from pathlib import Path
import sys,math
sys.path.insert(0,str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]/'authoring'))
from engine import Author
A=Author('25');add=A.add
add('5.6-01','Magnets have north and south poles; like poles repel and unlike poles attract.','They can attract suitable ferromagnetic material without that material initially being a permanent magnet.','Magnetic effects are often strongest near the poles. Cutting a bar magnet does not isolate an ordinary single pole: the pieces form smaller magnets with both poles in the school account. Attraction alone is therefore not sufficient evidence that an unknown object is a permanent magnet. Distinguish a magnetic pole from an electric charge sign.','State the interaction between two north poles.','They repel.')
add('5.6-02','Magnetisation can be modelled as increased alignment of microscopic magnetic dipoles.','In an unmagnetised ferromagnetic sample, differently oriented regions largely cancel their effects.','An external magnetic field can align domains more strongly, producing induced magnetism. Soft iron loses much of its induced magnetisation when the field is removed, while a suitable permanent-magnet material retains more. The dipoles are not separated electric charges moving from one end to the other.','Explain induced magnetism using dipole alignment.','An external field increases alignment of magnetic dipoles or domains.~Their effects cancel less, giving the material a net magnetic effect.')
add('5.6-03','A freely suspended magnet tends to align with the local magnetic field, including Earth’s field.','The end pointing toward magnetic north in this convention is called the north-seeking or north pole.','Keep strong nearby magnets, currents and magnetic objects away when demonstrating this alignment. Earth’s field is approximately bar-magnet-like for this school model, but magnetic and geographic directions are not identical everywhere. No current local compass correction is assumed here.','State why a freely suspended magnet can serve as a compass.','It aligns with the local magnetic field, usually dominated by Earth’s field.')
add('5.6-04','Ferrous materials contain iron; non-ferrous materials do not have iron as their defining base.','This composition classification is not identical to whether a sample is strongly magnetic.','Copper and aluminium are typical non-ferrous metals. Iron-based steels are ferrous, but alloy composition and structure influence their magnetic response. Nickel and cobalt can be ferromagnetic without being iron-based. Do not use the word ferrous as a complete synonym for every magnetic substance.','State the main composition distinction between ferrous and non-ferrous materials.','Ferrous materials are iron-containing or iron-based; non-ferrous materials are not iron-based.')
add('5.6-05','Iron, nickel and cobalt are important examples of ferromagnetic elements under ordinary conditions.','Many suitable alloys of these elements are also ferromagnetic, though alloy behaviour depends on composition and structure.','Ferromagnetic materials can show strong magnetic response and domain alignment. Some alloys do not retain the same response as their ingredients, so the example does not imply that every mixture containing any iron must behave identically. Steel is useful in permanent-magnet comparisons, while soft iron is useful in switched electromagnets.','State two ferromagnetic elements other than iron.','Nickel and cobalt.')
add('5.6-06','A plotting compass can map magnetic-field direction around a bar magnet.','Iron filings can reveal pattern shape but do not by themselves mark arrow direction.','Place the magnet under paper, mark its outline and poles, and use a small compass at successive positions to trace the north-tip direction. Repeat from different starting positions. Alternatively sprinkle filings lightly and tap the sheet gently to reveal alignment. Keep the compass sufficiently far from the magnet to rotate freely rather than being pulled into contact.','Describe how to map a bar magnet’s field direction.','Move a plotting compass between marked points and trace the direction indicated by its north-seeking end.')
add('5.6-07','External magnetic field lines run from a magnet’s north pole toward its south pole.','A horseshoe arrangement can produce an approximately uniform field in a narrow central gap.','Field lines form closed patterns including their continuation through the magnet. Denser line spacing represents stronger field qualitatively; it is not a literal collection of strings. Compare the curved external bar pattern with the more nearly parallel lines between nearby opposed pole faces.','Explain using a field diagram how a bar magnet’s external field differs from a horseshoe magnet’s central gap field.','Link the bar to curved external lines from north toward south.~The horseshoe’s central gap can have nearly parallel equally spaced lines between opposed poles.')
add('5.6-07-M01','A bar magnet has curved external field lines from N to S.','The field continues through the magnet from S to N in a closed pattern.','Draw several smooth noncrossing loops and add outside arrowheads from N to S. Lines emerge from different regions near the north pole and enter near the south. Close spacing near the ends indicates stronger field there; do not draw lines with two directions at the same point.','Draw the magnetic field around a horizontal bar magnet with N on the left and S on the right.','Draw smooth curved external lines from the left north pole to the right south pole.~Add N-to-S arrowheads outside and show denser spacing near the poles.')
bar=[dict(points=[[-1,-.25],[1,-.25],[1,.25],[-1,.25],[-1,-.25]],color='black')]
for h in [.65,1.1,1.6,-.65,-1.1,-1.6]:
 pts=[]
 for k in range(61):
  t=k/60;pts.append([-1+2*t,h*math.sin(math.pi*t)])
 bar.append(dict(points=pts,arrow=True))
bar.append(dict(points=[[.8,0],[-.8,0]],arrow=True,color='tab:red'))
A.geometry('5.6-07-M01',bar,labels=[[-.8,.05,'N'],[.65,.05,'S']],title='Bar magnet: schematic closed field pattern')
A.geometry('5.6-07',bar,labels=[[-.8,.05,'N'],[.65,.05,'S']],title='Bar pattern; compare with horseshoe gap below')
add('5.6-07-M02','Opposed nearby horseshoe pole faces can produce an approximately uniform central-gap field.','The field points from the N face to the S face.','Draw the U-shaped magnet with labelled faces and straight parallel evenly spaced lines across its central gap. Real edge regions curve, so do not extend the uniform assumption indefinitely outside the pole faces. Larger gap or irregular faces can make the simple model less accurate.','Draw the central field in a horseshoe magnet’s gap with its north face on the left and south face on the right.','Draw parallel approximately equally spaced lines across the central gap.~Put arrowheads from the north face toward the south face.')
A.geometry('5.6-07-M02',[dict(points=[[-1,.8],[-1,-1.5],[1,-1.5],[1,.8]],color='black')]+[dict(points=[[-.9,y],[.9,y]],arrow=True) for y in [0,.3,.6]],labels=[[-1.4,.9,'N'],[1.1,.9,'S']],title='Horseshoe magnet: central gap approximation')
add('5.6-08','A permanent magnet retains a substantial magnetic effect without a continuous electrical input.','An electromagnet uses current in a coil and can be switched or adjusted.','A soft-iron core strengthens a coil’s field and loses much of its induced effect after current stops. A bell needs repeated switching, making an electromagnet useful. A simple motor can combine a permanent field magnet with a current-carrying rotating coil. Neither device creates energy: a motor needs an input supply.','Explain why an electromagnet is useful in an electric bell.','Its magnetic attraction can be turned on and off by changing current.~A soft-iron core can lose much of its magnetisation when switched off, permitting the armature to return.')
add('5.7.1.1-01','A current-carrying straight wire and a solenoid produce magnetic fields.','Compass deflection near them can reveal the field and its reversal when current reverses.','Compare a compass with current off and then on in a low-voltage circuit. Keep the wire fixed and reverse connections to see a direction change. A solenoid gives a stronger organised pattern along its interior. Use brief currents to avoid heating and do not short the supply.','State an observation demonstrating a wire’s current-produced magnetic field.','A nearby compass changes direction when current is switched on and reverses its current-related deflection when current reverses.')
add('5.7.1.1-02','A straight wire gives circular field lines around its axis.','A solenoid gives an approximately uniform internal axial field and an external bar-like return pattern.','Use the right-hand grip rule with conventional current: thumb along wire current and fingers around the field. For a coil, curl fingers in the turn-current direction and the thumb points toward the north end. The two forms of the same rule connect current geometry to magnetic direction.','Explain using a field diagram how straight-wire and solenoid field patterns differ.','Link a straight wire to concentric circular field lines.~A solenoid combines its turns’ fields into an approximately parallel internal axial field with an external return.')
add('5.7.1.1-02-M01','A straight current-carrying wire has concentric circular field lines in a perpendicular cross-section.','Viewed with conventional current coming out of the page, the field is anticlockwise.','A dot indicates motion toward the viewer, like an arrow tip; a cross indicates away, like arrow feathers. Apply the right hand rather than the left-hand motor rule. Reversing current reverses circulation but not the circular shape.','Draw the field around a straight wire carrying conventional current out of the page.','Draw concentric circles centred on the wire.~Add anticlockwise arrows, consistent with an outward-current dot.')
segs=[]
for r in [.5,1,1.5]:
 pts=[[r*math.cos(t),r*math.sin(t)] for t in [i*2*math.pi/100 for i in range(101)]]
 segs.append(dict(points=pts));segs.append(dict(points=[[r*math.cos(.1),r*math.sin(.1)],[r*math.cos(.5),r*math.sin(.5)]],arrow=True))
A.geometry('5.7.1.1-02-M01',segs,labels=[[-.1,-.1,'⊙']],title='Out-of-page current: anticlockwise field')
A.geometry('5.7.1.1-02',segs,labels=[[-.1,-.1,'⊙']],title='Wire cross-section; solenoid diagram follows')
add('5.7.1.1-02-M02','A long solenoid has an approximately uniform field through its central interior.','Its field returns outside from the north end to the south end.','Viewed from a north end, conventional current around that end is anticlockwise. For a rightward internal field, label the right end N and left end S. A compass detects the field rather than the electron drift in each wire segment. Show exterior return curves to avoid implying the field stops at an end.','Draw a solenoid’s field with its north end on the right, showing interior and exterior directions.','Draw approximately parallel rightward field lines inside the coil.~Draw external return curves from the right north end toward the left south end.')
sol=[dict(points=[[-1.5,y],[1.5,y]],arrow=True) for y in [-.25,0,.25]]
for x in [-1.5,-1,-.5,0,.5,1,1.5]:sol.append(dict(points=[[x,-.5],[x,.5]],color='grey'))
for h in [1,-1]:sol.append(dict(points=[[1.5-3*k/60,h*math.sin(math.pi*k/60)] for k in range(61)],arrow=True))
A.geometry('5.7.1.1-02-M02',sol,labels=[[-1.8,-.8,'S'],[1.4,-.8,'N'],[-.6,1.2,'Outside return']],title='Solenoid: rightward interior field')
add('5.7.1.1-03','An electromagnet uses insulated wire wound around a suitable core.','Increasing current or suitable turn density can strengthen its field within the relevant operating range.','Wind a coil around soft iron and connect it to a rated low-voltage current-limited supply through a switch. Test attraction using the same target and contact geometry. Soft iron gives a switchable response; use insulation so adjacent turns do not short. Applications include relays, bells and lifting magnets.','Describe how to construct a simple classroom electromagnet.','Wind insulated wire around a soft-iron core and connect it through a switch to a suitable low-voltage current-limited supply.')
add('5.7.1.1-04','Soft iron magnetises and demagnetises relatively easily.','Steel generally retains more magnetisation after an applied field is removed in the intended comparison.','Use equal core shapes and the same coil current and turns. Compare attraction with current on, then after switching it off. A retained attraction in the steel sample contrasts with the soft iron’s reduced effect. Initial magnetisation, target contact and remanence matter, so repeat the sequence consistently.','Describe an experiment comparing iron and steel as electromagnet cores.','Use matched cores in the same coil, compare attraction with equal current, then compare retained attraction after switching off.')
add('5.7.1.1-05','A current-carrying wire can experience a force in a magnetic field.','Fleming’s left-hand rule relates field, conventional current and force for perpendicular directions.','First finger points along field from N to S, second finger along conventional current, and thumb along force. With field rightward and current out of the page, force is upward. Reversing either current or field reverses force; reversing both preserves it. A parallel current-field arrangement has no such perpendicular motor force in the elementary model.','Draw the force direction for a wire carrying conventional current out of the page in a rightward magnetic field.','Draw the field arrow rightward and mark current with an out-of-page dot.~Draw the force upward, using Fleming’s left-hand rule.')
A.geometry('5.7.1.1-05',[dict(points=[[-1.5,0],[1.5,0]],arrow=True),dict(points=[[0,0],[0,1.5]],arrow=True,color='tab:red')],labels=[[-.15,-.2,'⊙'],[.8,-.4,'B right'],[.1,1.4,'Force up'],[-1.5,-.8,'Current out of page']],title='Motor-force direction')
add('5.7.1.1-06','A moving charged-particle beam can be deflected by a magnetic field.','The force direction depends on charge sign, velocity direction and field direction.','In a teacher-operated enclosed electron-beam demonstration, compare the visible path with a magnetic field absent and present. Reversing the field reverses the deflection. An electron’s conventional-current direction is opposite its motion, so use that reversal with the left-hand rule. The apparatus can involve high voltage and is not constructed by learners.','Describe a demonstration that a magnetic field exerts a force on a moving electron beam.','Observe an enclosed teacher-operated beam before and after applying a transverse field, then reverse the field and compare its deflection.')
add('5.7.1.2-01','Opposite forces on the two active sides of a current-carrying coil can create a turning effect.','More turns, greater current or a stronger field can increase that effect with geometry and orientation fixed.','The forces form a couple rather than simply translating the whole coil. Coil orientation also matters, so comparisons must use the same angle and dimensions. The syllabus account is qualitative; no new force or torque equation is required. Connect each proposed change to increased total side forces rather than asserting an unlimited improvement.','Explain why more turns can increase a coil’s turning effect when current, field and geometry stay fixed.','Link each turn to an additional pair of side forces.~Their turning effects add, increasing the total moment within the operating range.')
add('5.7.1.2-01-M01','Opposite currents on opposite coil sides produce forces in opposite directions in a suitable field.','Their separated lines of action can form a turning couple.','Use the left-hand rule separately on each side. One side can be forced up while the other is forced down, giving a common sense of rotation about the axis. In some orientations the turning effect becomes small or zero; continuous rotation needs a suitable commutation arrangement and inertia.','Explain the origin of a current-carrying coil’s turning effect.','Opposite sides carry opposite currents and experience opposite forces.~Because the forces act at separated positions, they produce a turning couple.')
add('5.7.1.2-01-M02','Adding coil turns can increase the total turning effect at fixed current and field.','The contribution of multiple suitably aligned turns combines.','The comparison assumes the current is actually held fixed. Adding wire to a coil can increase resistance and reduce current from a fixed-voltage source, so turns alone do not guarantee the same measured change unless the conditions are controlled.','Explain how more turns can increase motor-coil torque at fixed current and geometry.','Each added turn contributes magnetic forces.~Their moments add to give a greater total turning effect.')
add('5.7.1.2-01-M03','Increasing coil current can increase magnetic side forces and turning effect.','The comparison holds field, geometry and orientation fixed within safe operating limits.','More current also increases heating, which can change resistance or damage the coil. A stronger measured turning tendency is therefore compared over a controlled range rather than extrapolated indefinitely. A current-limited supply supports a fair classroom test.','Explain why increasing current can make a motor coil turn more strongly.','The current-related magnetic forces on the coil sides increase.~Their couple therefore has a larger turning effect at the same geometry.')
add('5.7.1.2-01-M04','A stronger external magnetic field can increase the force on a given current-carrying coil.','At fixed orientation and geometry, this increases its turning effect.','A suitable core can concentrate the field, but comparisons must keep current and coil arrangement matched. Saturation and heating can limit a real system. The elementary qualitative relation does not mean the practical lifting-force data in this topic establish a universal force-current law.','Explain why a stronger field can increase motor-coil torque at unchanged current.','It increases the magnetic forces on the active coil sides.~The larger force pair produces a greater turning effect.')
add('5.7.1.2-02','A simple DC motor uses the coil’s magnetic turning effect to convert electrical input into rotation.','A split-ring commutator reverses coil current each half-turn in the basic single-coil arrangement.','Brushes connect the external DC supply to the rotating commutator halves. As the coil passes the appropriate orientation, the contacts swap the coil connections, preserving the torque’s rotational sense. Mechanical inertia helps carry the coil through a weak-torque position.','Explain how a simple single-coil DC motor maintains rotation.','The field exerts a turning couple on the current-carrying coil.~The split-ring commutator reverses coil current each half-turn so the couple continues in the same rotational sense.')
add('5.7.1.2-03','A suitable motor mechanism can operate as a generator when mechanical work rotates it.','The electrical supply is replaced by an output measurement or load in the intended demonstration.','Rotating conductors in the magnetic field induces an output; the mechanical input supplies the transferred energy. Merely disconnecting the supply does not produce sustained generation without driving the shaft. The output form depends on the collecting arrangement and is developed in the next topic.','Describe how to demonstrate a suitable small motor working as a generator.','Disconnect its power supply, connect a suitable output meter or load and drive the shaft mechanically.')
add('5.7.1.2-04','A split-ring commutator changes the coil’s connection to the DC supply every half-turn.','A soft-iron core strengthens the magnetic response of the coil arrangement.','The ring has separate conducting halves rather than two continuous slip rings. Brushes remain stationary while contacts rotate. Distinguish the connection-changing component from the core: the core does not reverse current, and the commutator does not create a stronger permanent field by itself.','Explain the different roles of a split-ring commutator and soft-iron coil core.','Link the split ring to current reversal each half-turn.~The soft-iron core strengthens the field interaction and can increase the turning effect.')
add('5.7.1.2-04-M01','Split-ring commutation reverses coil current at the appropriate half-turn positions.','This keeps a simple DC motor’s torque in the intended rotational sense.','Without reversal, after the coil passes the relevant alignment the forces can tend to turn it back. Continuous slip rings preserve the connection to each coil end and therefore do not provide the same DC-motor commutation action. Explain using changing side positions rather than saying the supply itself becomes AC.','Explain why a simple DC motor uses a split-ring commutator.','It reverses the coil current as the sides exchange positions each half-turn.~The force directions then preserve the rotational sense instead of opposing continued motion.')
add('5.7.1.2-04-M02','A suitable soft-iron core strengthens the coil’s magnetic field interaction.','Its easy magnetisation supports a stronger turning effect for the same basic supply conditions.','A cylindrical core also provides a practical winding support, but its magnetic role is the main explanation here. Do not confuse soft magnetic behaviour with physical softness or with a permanent magnet that remains strongly magnetised without the coil current.','Explain the magnetic purpose of a soft-iron core inside a motor coil.','The core magnetises readily and strengthens the relevant field.~This can increase forces and the turning effect on the coil.')
A.misconception('MIS-26','Attraction to a magnet alone does not establish that an unknown object is a permanent magnet.','An initially unmagnetised ferromagnetic object can be attracted by induced magnetism.','Test both ends of the object against a known pole while keeping other influences controlled. Repulsion supports the presence of a like pole on a magnetised object. It shows magnetisation at the time of the test; demonstrating long-term permanence also requires checking retention after other inducing influences are removed.','A bar is attracted to a known magnet. Explain why this observation alone does not prove it is a permanent magnet.','The bar may simply be unmagnetised ferromagnetic material attracted by induction.~Repulsion with a known pole would support a magnetic pole on the bar, and retention must be checked to establish permanence.')
C=A.choice
C('5.6-01','What do unlike magnetic poles do?',('Attract','N and S attract.'),('Repel','Like poles repel.'),('Become positive electric charge','Poles are not charge signs.'),('Lose all field by approaching','The field remains present.'))
C('5.6-02','What changes during magnetisation in the dipole model?',('Alignment increases','Net cancellation decreases.'),('Protons move to one end','That is not domain alignment.'),('The sample loses every atom','Matter remains.'),('Electrons become magnetic north only','Ordinary dipoles have both pole aspects.'))
C('5.6-05','Which pair is ferromagnetic under ordinary conditions?',('Nickel and cobalt','These are standard examples.'),('Copper and aluminium','They do not show the same strong ferromagnetism.'),('Glass and plastic','These are not the named ferromagnetic metals.'),('Water and air','These are not ferromagnetic examples.'))
C('5.6-08','Why is soft iron useful for a switched electromagnet?',('It magnetises and demagnetises readily','This suits on-off action.'),('It is a permanent electrical source','The coil needs input.'),('It has no magnetic response','Its response is the useful feature.'),('It reverses current mechanically','That is a commutator function.'))
C('5.7.1.1-02-M01','What shape is the field around a long straight wire?',('Concentric circles','They surround the current axis.'),('Straight radial electric lines','That is a different pattern.'),('Only one line along the wire','The magnetic field circles it.'),('No field outside the metal','A compass can detect it outside.'))
C('5.7.1.2-04-M01','What does a split-ring commutator do in the simple motor?',('Reverses coil current each half-turn','This preserves the rotational torque sense.'),('Increases the number of turns','It changes connections, not winding count.'),('Stores fuel','It is an electrical contact arrangement.'),('Reverses the external permanent poles each half-turn','The coil connection is reversed instead.'))
C('5.7.1.2-03','What input does a generator require?',('Mechanical work','This supplies its electrical output energy.'),('No input','That would create energy.'),('Only a disconnected wire','A changing field linkage is needed.'),('A vanishing magnetic field everywhere','A useful field interaction is needed.'))
C('5.6-01','An object is attracted to a magnet. What can be concluded?',('It may be magnetised or merely magnetisable','Attraction alone cannot distinguish these.'),('It must be a permanent magnet','Induced attraction is an alternative.'),('It must be non-ferrous copper','That is not supported.'),('It must repel the other pole','Unmagnetised iron can be attracted to either.'))
C('5.7.1.1-05','Current is out of the page and field rightward. Which way is force?',('Upward','The left-hand rule gives this direction.'),('Downward','That reverses one required direction.'),('Along the current','Force is perpendicular in this setup.'),('Along the field','It is perpendicular to the field.'))
C('5.7.1.1-02-M01','Reverse a straight wire’s current. What happens to its magnetic field?',('Circular direction reverses','The right-hand rule changes sense.'),('Circles become straight radial lines','Shape remains circular.'),('It becomes an electric field by definition','These are different fields.'),('It disappears despite current','A field remains with reversed direction.'))
C('5.7.1.2-01-M02','More turns are added but current falls. What is needed for the simple turns comparison?',('Hold current and other relevant conditions fixed','Otherwise two effects change together.'),('Assume torque must increase by any amount','Current reduction can matter.'),('Ignore winding resistance','It can alter current.'),('Remove the field','That removes the intended interaction.'))
C('5.7.1.2-01','A short empirical test gives lifting force 0.4 N at 0.2 A and 0.8 N at 0.4 A. What is the secant gradient?',('2 N/A','Force change 0.4 N divided by current change 0.2 A.'),('0.5 A/N','This is the reciprocal.'),('0.4 N/A','This omits the current interval.'),('0.08 N/A','This multiplies changes.'))
P=A.part
P('5.6-01','State the interaction of like poles.','Repulsion.',1,'One mark for repel.')
P('5.6-05','Name a ferromagnetic element other than iron.','Nickel.',1,'One mark for nickel or cobalt.')
P('5.6-02','Explain magnetisation in the dipole model.','More dipoles or domains align. Their fields cancel less and give a net magnetic effect.',2,'One mark for alignment; one for net effect.')
P('5.7.1.2-04-M01','Explain why the coil current reverses each half-turn in a simple DC motor.','The coil sides exchange positions. Reversing current keeps the torque in the same rotational sense.',2,'One mark for changed side positions; one for preserved torque sense.')
P('5.7.1.2-01-M03','Explain the expected effect of increased current at fixed coil geometry and external field.','Magnetic side forces increase. Their couple produces a larger turning effect within the operating range.',2,'One mark for larger forces; one for larger moment.')
A.deviations.append(dict(from_='Two numerical structured applications',to='Commutation and controlled motor-effect applications.',why='The syllabus teaches these effects qualitatively; no extra force or torque law is introduced.'))
A.practical=dict(graph='Plot lifting force vertically against coil current horizontally; fit the provided empirical range without forcing the line through zero.',unit='N/A',meaning='The gradient is an empirical change in lifting force per current over this stated range. It is not a universal magnetic-force constant or proof of a general linear law.',method=['Wind an insulated coil on a soft-iron core and connect a current-limited low-voltage supply through an ammeter and switch.','Use a force gauge to measure the force needed to detach the same target at a fixed contact geometry.','Set a small current and record it with the corresponding detachment force.','Repeat at several safe currents, switching off between readings to limit warming.','Repeat each force reading after restoring the same target position and core state as closely as possible.'],control='Keep turns, core, gap, target and alignment fixed; limit heating and prevent the target falling onto hands or feet.',table='Record current / A, repeated detachment or lifting force / N and mean force / N.',conclusion='Describe the measured increase over the tested range and obtain its empirical slope; do not extrapolate the invented straight-line data into a universal magnetic law.')
if __name__=='__main__':A.build()
